Revert upstream commit 6bee0c43b24a09213b1a69451e1a3ca46e529fe3
("session: Set GDM_AUTH_SESSION_ID env var for PAM conversations"),
included in the 51.beta tarball (committed 2026-08-04, one day before
this release was cut). It breaks interactive login entirely:

Symptom: after upgrading to gdm-51_beta, the greeter shows the user
list fine, but activating a username (mouse or keyboard) does nothing
-- no password prompt, no gdm-password worker, no journal event at
all for the activation. Only visible symptom is at greeter startup:

  gdm-launch-environment][PID]: PAM pam_putenv: delete non-existent
  entry; GDM_AUTH_SESSION_ID
  gdm-launch-environment][PID]: Gdm: cannot put GDM_AUTH_SESSION_ID
  in pam environment: Bad item passed to pam_*_item()

Root cause: the commit sets GDM_AUTH_SESSION_ID via a plain setenv()
in gdm_session_worker_initialize_pam() (daemon/gdm-session-worker.c),
i.e. only in the worker's own process environment, never through
pam_putenv(). Later, gdm_session_worker_authenticate_user() tries to
remove it via gdm_session_worker_set_environment_variable(worker,
GDM_AUTH_SESSION_ID, NULL), which calls pam_putenv(handle,
"GDM_AUTH_SESSION_ID") to delete it from the PAM environment -- an
entry that was never added there in the first place, since the setup
step used setenv() instead of pam_putenv(). This mismatch is present
for every session conversation now (the commit also moved the
"session-id-of-caller" assignment in gdm-manager.c's
on_session_conversation_started() ahead of the gdm-autologin check,
so it runs unconditionally, not just for autologin), and something in
that broadened codepath stops the greeter from ever starting a new
gdm-password conversation when a user is activated.

This is a same-day regression with no upstream follow-up fix yet as
of 2026-08-05 (verified: it is the newest commit in daemon/ touching
this code). Reverting it restores gdm-51_beta to working login while
keeping the prerequisite refactor from commit bbf4a3a065cd9705ab
("session: Add \"session-id-of-caller\" property") intact, since that
one is a self-contained, harmless rename from a GObject data field to
a proper property and does not itself introduce the bug.

